      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, without any extra over-hangs and without gaps, we need to find the smallest square that can be covered by these rectangles. The key is to find a side length \\( n \\) such that a square of side length \\( n \\) can be divided into smaller squares of side lengths 2 and 3 without gaps or overlaps.\n\nFirst, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of 2 and 3. The LCM of 2 and 3 is 6. This means that a square of side length 6 can be divided into 3 smaller squares of side length 2 and 1 smaller square of side length 3, or 2 smaller squares of side length 2 and 2 smaller squares of side length 3, or 1 smaller square of side length 6.\n\nWe will consider the case where the square is divided into 3 smaller squares of side length 2 and 1 smaller square of side length 3. The area of the large square is \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\) square units. The area of one 2-by-3 rectangle is \\( 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\) square units. Therefore, the number of rectangles needed is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36}{6} = 6\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can check that a 6-by-6 square can indeed be covered by 6 rectangles of 2-by-3 dimensions without any gaps or overlaps. We can visualize or physically arrange the rectangles to confirm this.\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
