      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we first need to calculate the area of the square and the area of one 2-by-3 rectangle. The area of the square is given by $6 \\times 6 = 36$ square units. The area of one 2-by-3 rectangle is $2 \\times 3 = 6$ square units. Therefore, the number of 2-by-3 rectangles required to cover the square exactly is the area of the square divided by the area of one rectangle, rounded up to the nearest whole number because we can't use a fraction of a rectangle.\n\nThe calculation is as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of rectangles} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{36}{6} \\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil 6 \\right\\rceil = 6\n\\]\nHere, $\\left\\lceil x \\right\\rceil$ denotes the ceiling function, which rounds $x$ up to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is $\\boxed{6}$.",
